today I'm going to explain the  Heisenberg uncertainty principle and in  case you haven't heard of it the most  common version of the Heisenberg  uncertainty basically says that you  can't know a particles position and its  momentum both perfectly instead the more  you know about one the less you can know  about the other today we're going to  figure out what that actually means if  you remember way back to the first  episode I introduced you to the wave  function which is a wave that tells us  where the particle is most likely to  turn up we said that a particle is most  likely to turn up in areas around where  the wave function is most intense so  from the wave function you can find out  the place that on average most particles  will turn up but of course the average  by itself isn't that useful we'd also  like to know the range around the  average that the particles are most  likely to fall into well for a peak like  this it looks like the particles will  most likely turn up in this kind of  region to calculate this precisely  though we use what's called the standard  deviation this number also gets called  the uncertainty because the bigger it is  the more uncertain we are about where  the particle will be now let's talk  about momentum we know that while we're  not looking a particle must take all  possible Momentum's at once if we plot  all the different momentum so particle  has that also looks like a wave we'll  call this wave the momentum wavefunction  just like with the other wave function  the Momentum's where the wave is most  intense are the more likely to be  measured Momentum's at the moment we  don't know how to find this wave but  we'll get to that later  in the meantime we'll define uncertainty  for the momentum as well in the exact  same way we did with position again this  uncertainty tells us that if you do a  measurement on the particles momentum  you're most likely to find it in that  region so now the Heisenberg uncertainty  is the claim that somehow these two  uncertainties are connected in fact if  you multiply the two the number you get  will always be bigger than this tiny  number here so what does this actually  mean imagine we managed to get the  particle into a state like this now  we're pretty sure about where the  particle will end up however as a result  we don't know very well what momentum  we'll get if we measure it so you see  the uncertainty principle is all about  prediction it restricts how well we can  predict the particles momentum and  position at the same time let's look at  a really interesting example say we go  ahead and measure the particles position  what happens if we decide to measure the  particles position again straight  afterwards well remember that before we  did our measurement the particle was in  a superposition of being in many places  at once  but once we measure it we break that  superposition and now the particle  really is in just one place so if we  measure it again quickly it will in fact  be in the exact same spot okay but what  if we're going to measure the particles  momentum after we measure its position  now your uncertainty of our position is  practically zero so your uncertainty  about momentum is very big this means it  will be really hard to guess the  particles momentum still we go ahead and  we measure the momentum and we get some  number but wait now it looks like we  know the position and the momentum of  the particle perfectly well and doesn't  the uncertainty principle forbid that  well are we sure we know the particles  position what happens if we measure it  again and check the overwhelming chances  are that it will be somewhere other than  where it was the first time why because  by measuring the momentum we actually  change the particles position  wavefunction the particle went from  being in pretty much just one spot to  being in many different spots so you can  see we can never pin down both the  momentum and the position of the  particle why is that the uncertainty  principle isn't just something that we  add into quantum mechanics in an ad hoc  way the reason it happens is because of  a special relationship between the  position wavefunction and the momentum  wavefunction well firstly it's weird  that there should be any relationship  between them at all usually with the  the object's velocity is independent of  its position by that I mean an object  can be moving at all different speeds  regardless of where it is in quantum  mechanics that's just not the case the  position wavefunction actually contains  all the information about the momentum  wavefunction in fact it contains all the  information about the particles state in  general say we're interested in  measuring something else about the  particle like its energy angular  momentum or in the double slit  experiment case which letter went  through you can calculate what values  are most likely to turn up just from the  position wavefunction in other words in  quantum mechanics the wavefunction tells  us everything there is to know about a  particle still there is a particularly  special relationship between momentum  and position that makes the uncertainty  principle happen I'd like to explain it  to you but it's a bit of a detour so I'm  not sure if all of you want to see it  that's why I've decided to make two  short supplementary episodes that will  explain it if you're interested you can  check them out they'll be up soon all  right  see you then
 

